{"case_id": 2909, "Source": "Malaysia Airlines Flight 17", "Index": 104, "Text": "Dutch Prime Minister Mark Rutte and King Willem-Alexander voiced their shock at the crash, and Minister of Foreign Affairs Frans Timmermans joined the Dutch investigation team sent to Ukraine. Dutch government buildings flew the flag at half-mast on 18 July. Music was cancelled and festivities were toned down on the last day of the Nijmegen Marches. On 21 July the Netherlands opened a war crimes investigation on the downing of the aircraft and a Netherlands public prosecutor went to Ukraine as part of this investigation. Rutte threatened tough action against Russia if it did not help in the investigation. On the same day, Timmermans spoke at the UN Security Council Meeting, after the council had unanimously condemned the shooting down of MH17. An increase in negative emotions and somatic complaints was observed in the Dutch population during the first four days after the MH17 crash. Russell Shorto of Time wrote that due to the small size of the Netherlands, \"everyone is within a couple of degrees of separation of everyone else\". Shorto noted: \"the proportionate loss of life for a country the size of the United States would be about 6,000 people\".", "entities": [{"entity": "Dutch", "label": "MISC", "start": 0, "end": 5, "origin": "human_confirmed_flertdate"}, {"entity": "Mark Rutte", "label": "PER", "start": 21, "end": 31, "origin": "human_confirmed_flertdate"}, {"entity": "Willem-Alexander", "label": "PER", "start": 41, "end": 57, "origin": "human_added_or_corrected"}, {"entity": "Frans Timmermans", "label": "PER", "start": 123, "end": 139, "origin": "human_confirmed_flertdate"}, {"entity": "Dutch", "label": "MISC", "start": 151, "end": 156, "origin": "human_confirmed_flertdate"}, {"entity": "Ukraine", "label": "LOC", "start": 184, "end": 191, "origin": "human_confirmed_flertdate"}, {"entity": "Dutch", "label": "MISC", "start": 193, "end": 198, "origin": "human_confirmed_flertdate"